WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.240
Film Courage: Why do writers get stuck?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.750
Alan Watt, Author, Screenwriter and Founder
of L.A. Writers' Lab: Why do writers get stuck?

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.830
Well, you want to have a good outline.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:17.070
Writers gets stuck sometime because they come
up with a great idea and they immediately

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:24.060
tart writing the idea which is fine but at
some point you are going to want to stop and

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.470
do some kind of outline.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.320
And here is the thing.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.610
This is where writers go “Yeah but I heard
that Woody Allen never outlined.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.040
Charlie Kaufman never outlines.”

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.590
And the truth is they do, they probably just
done write their outline down.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:48.140
They have been writing stories for such a
long time and they probably do have lots of

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:56.760
notes and they have probably developed such
a…story structure becomes second nature

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.760
to them.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:09.630
They’ve become masters and so they are naturally
connected to this process.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:15.711
But I would think the main reason writers
get stuck is they are not working off of some

00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:16.711
kind of an outline.

00:01:16.711 --> 00:01:23.549
It could be the most rudimentary outline but
you’ve got to have something to work from.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:30.619
Or the irony is if you don’t have an outline
you are actually going to lose confidence

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:34.780
when you get to a point where you start to
feel stuck.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.499
Because you are always going to get stuck
at some point in the process but if you’ve

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:49.000
got some point of basic sense of the story
structure, basic sense of the movement you

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.280
can actually get excited about the point that
you get stuck because you know where the story

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:02.979
is going to go and that’s where you can
kind of relax into the panic and let your

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:09.550
characters speak to you and go in directions
that you never imagined that are actually

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.530
going to support where the story wants to
go.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.580
That’s why I always say if you know you’re
going to go left, go right.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.110
So if I know this is going to happen, let
me explore the opposite and relax in the fact

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:28.600
that I’m going to get to where I’m going
it’s just that I’m not going to arrive

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.350
there the same way I thought I was going to.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:36.740
Film Courage: I’m thinking of like THE FIRM
with Tom Cruise…well I don’t want to give

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.740
any spoilers away.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.620
Alan: I don’t think you are giving any spoilers
away to a 30 year movie.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.180
If you haven’t watched THE FIRM yet?

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.230
Film Courage: Okay…so I’m just thinking
of the living room scene where he comes back

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.310
and the partners are there and maybe I’m
forgetting this part?

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:53.310
Alan: Yes!

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.900
And totally different…this is where Robert
Towne…I love this!

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:05.900
Robert Towne adapted THE FIRM and he…I always
thought it was a great novel except the ending

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:13.660
felt like a total cheat because Mitch McDeere
ends up on a sailboat with 10 million dollars

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:19.030
and he’s in like the Cayman Islands or something
and he’s not a lawyer any more but he wanted

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.030
to be a lawyer.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.440
He loved being a lawyer.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.230
And so Robert Towne he read it and said “Wait
a minute?

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:31.150
Why can’t he be a lawyer at the end of the
story?

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.610
But have a transformation?”

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.330
So in the living room scene (it’s been decades
since I’ve seen this movie).

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:48.090
But in the living room scene is where he confronts
is it the mobsters and the people from his

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.140
law firm?

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.820
Film Courage: Maybe…and isn’t is wife
there?

00:03:52.820 --> 00:04:01.740
Alan: But what he basically does is he says
“I’m a lawyer and I work for you but I

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:10.900
also have all of your stuff and if anything
happens to me you guys are messed up.”

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.560
And so that’s what John Grisham didn’t
do.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.310
But that’s what makes Robert Towne a genius
is because he understands story structure,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.360
he understands transformation.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.620
I mean Josh Grisham is brilliant, I love John
Grisham.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:38.180
But the ending of that felt like a cop-out
to me because he just goes of…that ending

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.610
is almost a tragedy.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:44.870
He’s going to spend the rest of his life
in hiding (in the novel) in hiding in the

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.569
Cayman Islands with 10 million dollars.

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:50.879
That sounds like a nightmare!

00:04:50.879 --> 00:04:57.189
Who wants that life because he’s in hiding?

00:04:57.189 --> 00:05:09.490
But the way Robert Towne did it was masterful
because he was true to the dramatic question

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:16.900
about a guy who was an idealistic lawyer who
takes a job for a “dirty firm” and ends

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:27.129
up in a horror show and he manages a way to
climb out and outsmart the villains.

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:28.029
Brilliant!

